{"kind":"report","text":"A1c pre-Source decision against Main b686384a813b7ed607ec88ee263cc4fb27157998; no Source approval or authority to publish bytes.\n\nReuse the replay consumer for its dependency reads, with the following corrections before a manifest can claim completeness.\n\n1. Freeze the actual scope incarnation, cutoff position/hash, pinned code/declaration versions, bounds, export-policy version and any prior archived prefix. A HistorySource wrapper must enforce the cutoff in head, entries and pagination; Options.head only checks an anchor and does not truncate traversal (verify.ts run/#open/#stored). A page past the cutoff must not enter the selected history. Foreign history traversal is separately bounded and identified; its read-set is not silently copied into this scope's inventory.\n\n2. Record successful, validated dependencies, not every attempted retained request. #hand tries the same observation digest in multiple retained domains until one validates; failed probes are not all mandatory inputs or omissions. Key by scope incarnation/kind/domain/digest, retain required under metadata, check bytes/metadata/digest, deduplicate deterministically and charge aggregate bytes/entries/scopes/depth/work. A retained foreign entry copy does not recursively bring its own retained inputs into this archive. Proof dependencies on its source history remain separately explicit.\n\n3. The current verifier is not the whole 15.3 inventory oracle. #pin validates one declaration; #replay act places read directly named declarations, but verify.ts does not follow create-send declarations to transitive closure. Runtime packages/scope/src/definitions.ts creates/namedBy supplies that separate consumer. The inventory must also cover and bound the declaration closure promised in15.3 (including unused named child declarations), sharing the actual declaration-consumer rules rather than quietly dropping this row. Runtime namedBy tolerates absent child declarations, so its ok result alone does not certify the archive's full closure. Missing/unsupported closure must be explicit and cannot yield complete.\n\n4. No early stop, source error, missing/invalid non-text dependency, unsupported consumer or exhausted limit may return an apparently complete inventory. Coverage alone is insufficient. The final unwalked-ancestry Stop occurs after target advancement, head validation and #settle, so that particular typed terminal phase may yield complete selected retained inventory while replay remains incomplete for the unwalked commits. Do not infer this from prose why or from status=incomplete alone. Detached text is always omitted, but unmodified verify also reads it and #settle may advance histories to their heads looking for tombstones. Missing text can stop an otherwise traversed inventory; a source recorder cannot simply declare success. Either conservatively retain incomplete here or provide an explicit bounded dependency-inventory phase sharing consumers, with separate completion/proof/text-omission states. Do not invent source text, anchor foreign histories, use integrity mode or weaken replay judgment to force success.\n\nPrivacy decision: deny by default before writing any object, ref, diagnostic payload or policy-sensitive manifest field. Service read permission and a domain name are not export permission. Classification must cover the entire canonical native entry (signed intents, fields, prepared results, effects, sends and embedded copies), retained bytes, under metadata, paths and omission metadata. A reversible path encoding or guessable digest does not make private data safe.\n\nThe concrete initial policy is: text never exported; generic declarations/custom domains, unknown pins/input variants and foreign entry kinds have no implicit allowance. Permit only an explicit reviewed finite policy bound to exact definition/owner versions and byte schemas, after repository-readability disclosure and room consent. There is currently no reviewed safe-cohort list in this decision. Until it exists, a pure inventory/classification helper can be built, but no first writer is authorized. An excluded retained dependency can be an explicit omission only when its omission metadata is itself safe; clone replay must remain incomplete where required input is excluded. If metadata is unsafe, stop. Unknown/private native entry content stops the pass before any publication: do not skip, redact, re-sign or rewrite it. A stopped pass does not advance archivedThrough or claim a complete native prefix.\n\nThis exposes an owner-level contradiction in15.3/J2/J3, not an implementation detail: membership join/enrol secrets are inline text fields (membership.ts385,404) in retained signed intents. The contract simultaneously requires every unchanged entry and excludes credentials/private bytes. Repository-wide consent does not resolve that stated exclusion. Membership histories and any retained copy carrying such an entry are not approved for export, even if a credential has expired. Planner must amend the archive/privacy contract or design a versioned credential-safe native history before those cohorts are enabled. Full generic/custom-domain and planned membership archive behavior remains owed; a finite helper slice cannot satisfy full A1/A2.\n\nJ1 is a conditional deployed-default proof only: canonical entryBytes is bounded before append and the current deployment uses 256KiB, below8MiB. Custom Bounds remain possible; the archive still refuses a single oversized line.\n\nEvidence: static read of exact Main replay run, #pin, #retained/#text/#hand/#snapshots/#replay, HistorySource/RetainedInput, runtime definition closure, membership fields and read-session filters; reviewed note15.3/J1/J2/J3. No project execution or private evidence read.","body":{"formal_approval":"false","promise":"git:sha1:589bca982736a2361d15d53f7572375ef2f7aaf3#git:sha1:273443b0f2934129ce77e47cdcf1e60b2dc0377a","scope":"pre-Source inventory/privacy dependency decision only"}}